WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.490
JENNY BROWN: Welcome to Cloud
Security Basics, a series where

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:06.820
we explain the ins and outs
of securing your application

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.650
on Google Cloud.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:09.790
Sound fun?

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:10.720
Then stick around.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.520
Because in this
episode, you'll learn

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.890
about Google Cloud
service accounts

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:17.544
and why you might
want to be using them.

00:00:17.544 --> 00:00:20.508
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:34.834
[PHONE DIALING]

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:38.260
SPEAKER: Hello, Cloud detective.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.140
Last time, you foiled my plan
to compromise your company's

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.280
security.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.310
But you used IAM to keep
your members identity secure

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:48.880
and keep me from accessing
the data I wanted.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.380
Your employees
are secure enough,

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.730
but what about your
scripts and applications?

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:56.740
If the applications
that can automatically

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.900
access your resources
can be compromised,

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.530
then so can a lot of your
company's information.

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:05.560
JENNY BROWN: OK, so
here's what we know.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.430
Many times, it's useful to have
an application or script that

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:13.180
can access resources
like data storage buckets

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:16.720
or other Cloud
resources on your behalf

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.600
without needing a person
to manually sign off

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.520
on the access.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.420
One way of doing this is by
storing a user's credentials

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:28.660
and letting the automation
script or application use

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:29.950
those.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.740
But there are a lot of
pitfalls involved when

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.990
working with user credentials.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:38.260
For example, if I'm
using my user credentials

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.380
to access the resources
in my automation script,

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.840
then any time I share
access to the script,

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.930
I'll have to share my
credentials with someone else.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.000
And this opens up
the possibility

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.770
of someone using my credentials
for something I didn't intend.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:57.370
And since I'd also have to
store my credentials somewhere

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.770
for my script to be
able to use them,

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:02.320
this adds another security risk.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.310
What if the wrong person
gets access to those secrets?

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:09.070
So ideally, I'd like
an automated way

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:12.130
to share access to my
Google Cloud resources

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:17.360
without having to store or share
my user account credentials.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.110
Luckily, Google Cloud provides
a lot of tools and services

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:24.360
to protect users from
this exact problem.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.690
And in this case,
Google service accounts

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:29.000
could avoid many
of these issues.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.390
For example, instead of just
giving out my user credentials

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:35.420
to run workloads on
VMs, it would be better

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:38.030
to create a service
account for the application

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:41.270
and grant it access
to call Google APIs

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:44.360
so that users don't even
need to be involved.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.000
Then I could grant
groups of people access

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.800
to that service account.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.130
And this access can be
easily revoked if necessary

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:54.290
and doesn't involve
sharing user credentials

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:56.510
to access a resource.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:58.940
But how should I set them up?

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.930
Let's think about this.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:05.900
What else do I know about
using service accounts?

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:09.020
A service account
is a robot identity,

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.060
but don't tell them that.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.890
And this service
account identity

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:15.140
is assigned to
permissions and roles

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:19.310
in order to access resources
and set credentials the same way

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.510
you'd grant access to humans.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:25.340
So because service accounts
provide the same access

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.360
as user accounts, it would be
wise to significantly limit

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.270
scope and review who
has account permissions

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:35.690
and access to your accounts
on a regular basis.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:37.880
Service accounts
don't use passwords

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:40.700
in the same way a
user account might.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.800
So if you're within
Google Cloud,

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.680
then you can just attach
service accounts to instances

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.410
and other resources
within Google Cloud.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:51.170
And it'll securely take care
of authenticating the identity

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:53.300
of the service account.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.450
But if you're using service
accounts in another Cloud

00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:00.980
platform or on prem, you can
use public and private keys

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:03.540
to authenticate those
service accounts.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:05.600
And if you're using
a service key,

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:07.820
it's important to
not download them

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:10.940
because then you've gone from
an identity problem, which

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:13.640
is what service accounts
are intended to solve,

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:17.029
to a secrets management
problem like, who

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:18.800
has access to that key file?

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.750
And how will that
access be controlled?

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:25.610
This is why some organizations
set up policy disabling account

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:27.390
creation entirely.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:30.860
And if you give an individual
your service account key code,

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:33.020
it's really not so different
from giving someone

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:35.480
your username and
password to your account.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:39.540
It grants them access to
whatever you have access to.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:44.110
So if we decide to
use keys, I want

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:49.760
to, well, one make a policy that
we store them somewhere secure

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.000
and that we don't do something
like commit the service account

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:56.360
credentials to the repository
with the source code, which

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:58.760
would defeat a lot of
the benefits of service

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:03.340
accounts, two, look
at key rotation.

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:06.700
A security best practice is
to rotate service account keys

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:07.780
regularly.

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:10.030
And this just involves
creating a new key,

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:13.540
switching apps to the new key,
and then deleting the old key.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:15.910
But it helps a lot with
securing our credentials

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:19.270
as long as they're stored
and accessed securely.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:22.900
Still, that doesn't prevent
the very human errors

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:26.440
like assigning the wrong service
accounts to the wrong users

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:30.790
or applications, which would
grant unnecessary permissions.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:34.090
So to minimize the
chance of that happening

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:37.660
as much as possible,
just like with IAM,

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:40.840
it would be wise to use a naming
convention for your service

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:41.920
accounts.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.200
It should be easy to
look at a service account

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:46.330
and know its purpose.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:55.200
A naming pattern like
that could be effective

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:58.080
here as long as the
service account name fits

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:00.700
into the 30-character limit.

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:04.530
So if a Cloud team requires
BigQuery and Cloud Storage

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:07.830
access to automate
data uploads, we

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:14.070
would provision a service
account named that.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:17.910
And finally, I want
to implement a policy

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:21.600
of tracking and logging
unused service accounts.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.720
So hopefully, we have some
logging and monitoring set up

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.390
because a single application
can have hundreds

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:29.550
of service accounts if needed.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:31.860
And as these
continue to pile up,

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:35.210
admins may start to
lose track of them all.

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:39.230
Maybe it would be wise to create
a policy to disable service

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:41.750
accounts that haven't
been used in a while

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:44.360
or at the least have someone
manually take a look at them

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:47.060
to see if they're still valid.

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:48.200
That's it.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:51.050
I think I have enough
info to set this up.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:52.790
SPEAKER: Egads, Detective.

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:56.780
I see you solved yet another
Cloud Security riddle of mine.

00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:00.020
I was hoping to prey on
the automated access points

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.630
into your Cloud
infrastructure by searching

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:05.540
for stored tokens, passwords,
and credentials and source

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:08.810
repos, but I didn't find any.

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:10.325
It seems like
you're using Cloud--

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:16.160
it seems like you're
using Google Cloud service

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:17.610
accounts to keep
your application

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:20.180
secure and scalable.

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:22.880
Service accounts can
thwart people like me,

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:25.610
allow access to the right
identities when necessary,

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:28.520
and allow applications
to access GCP resources

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.770
without human intervention.

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:33.530
They make it possible to
efficiently and quickly set up

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:36.290
permissions, scopes, and
roles for your organization

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:39.270
to keep people like me out.

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:41.870
I hate to admit this, but you
should use service accounts

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:44.420
if you need to authenticate
applications or scripts to use

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:47.390
additional Google
Cloud Services.

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:49.310
You can use them to
give out permissions

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:51.680
across numerous
resources without having

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:54.920
to expose user data and possibly
negatively impact your app

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:57.410
security.

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:00.890
But you can't stop every
violation from occurring.

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:02.060
Be ready.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:04.610
Because next, we'll
find out, what

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:09.003
will you do when something
sneaks past your defenses?

00:08:09.003 --> 00:08:10.670
Hopefully, you're
monitoring and logging

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:12.470
what's happening in
your application,

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:17.190
or else you have no idea what
to do when something goes wrong.

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:19.400
JENNY BROWN: So there you
have it, another episode

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.500
of Cloud Security Basics.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:23.780
Next episode, we'll
focus on using

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:27.860
logging and auditing to track
down suspicious activities.

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:30.800
So stay tuned for the rest
of the Cloud Security Basics

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:31.640
series.

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:33.710
Because when it
comes to security,

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:35.600
you can't let bad actors win.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:38.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

